{"name":"Soliflore fragrances","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg/960px-Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg","caption":"Soliflore en émail de Longwy 268.2 03","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A soliflore is a fragrance dominated by the scent of one particular flower, such as rose, carnation or iris; the word is French. Perfume classification lists these as the single floral family, in contrast with floral bouquets that blend several flowers.","For grooming, a single-flower scent offers a simple, recognisable smell that is easy to pair with an outfit or occasion. The appearance payoff is indirect: scent is part of overall presentation rather than visible looks."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People wear it as they would any perfume, applying it to skin where it develops through top, heart and base notes over hours. Some layer it with other products, and soliflores are often chosen for a distinct signature rather than complexity."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Fragrance can cause skin irritation or allergy in some people, and how long and how strongly a perfume lasts varies with skin dryness, diet and medication."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg/960px-Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Soliflore_en_%C3%A9mail_de_Longwy_268.2_03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Soliflore en émail de Longwy 268.2 03","license":"CC BY-